<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # A Vessel Is Crossing! – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting the Panama Canal</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> A Vessel Is Crossing!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: communication: Assist classmates in interpreting homework assignments. concept: Compare a simple classroom object to something familiar.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can assist classmates with Canal-related homework by pointing to relevant visuals (e.g., pointing to the Panama Canal on the map and saying, 'This is the Canal.'). Can compare the Panama Canal to a familiar object using gestures (e.g., 'The Canal is like a big water road.' and 'draws a line.'). Can facilitate peer communication by mimicking (e.g., pretends to steer a vessel for 'vessel in the Panama Canal.').
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large map of Panama with the Canal highlighted and pictures of different vessels crossing the Canal (boat, ship, yacht). The teacher points to key features and asks simple questions, modeling target vocabulary. "Look at this map. This is the Panama Canal. What do you see here? (Points to a vessel). Yes, a vessel! It's crossing the canal."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models explaining a simple fact about the Canal, using gestures. The teacher points to the map and says, "The Panama Canal connects two oceans," then uses hands to show a connection. The teacher also models mimicking steering a boat for "vessel in the Panama Canal." Pronunciation focus: Teacher clearly articulates /k/ in 'Canal' and /b/ in 'boat'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses CCQs to check understanding of key words and concepts.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the Canal connect two cities or two oceans?" (Expected answer: "Two oceans!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a yacht a small car or a type of boat?" (Expected answer: "A type of boat!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting the Panama Canal"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated dialogue between two friends, Ana and Carlos, discussing their "Virtual Canal Tour" project. The dialogue shows one friend explaining a picture of the Canal to the other, clarifying details, and using gestures. For example, Ana points to a picture: "This is the Canal. It connects the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans. It's very important." Carlos asks, "What is important?" Ana replies, "It's important because many ships travel through it every day. Look, this big ship is transporting goods." Ana draws a line in the air, "The Canal is like a big water road."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing facts (e.g., 'The Panama Canal connects two oceans.') and Comparing things (e.g., 'That black vessel is bigger than the white ship.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the dialogue and respond to gist questions.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are Ana and Carlos talking about?" (Their project, the Panama Canal)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does Ana say the Canal is like?" (A big water road)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Mediation: Assisting classmates in interpreting, comparing using gestures, and facilitating peer communication. Grammar: Present simple (facts), Present continuous (current events), Comparatives (bigger, smaller). Vocabulary: canal, vessel, ocean, locks, connect, big, small, travel, observe.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Match the Explanation."</b> The teacher provides sentence strips with simple Canal facts or questions (e.g., "What connects the oceans?") and corresponding picture cards (e.g., map of the Canal, picture of locks, a vessel). Students work in pairs to match the sentences to the correct pictures. One student points to the picture and says, "This is the Canal. It connects two oceans." The other student listens and confirms.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Gesture & Compare."</b> The teacher displays various pictures of vessels (a small boat, a large ship, a yacht) and a classroom object (e.g., a long ruler). Students practice comparing. Teacher provides sentence frames: "This _____ is bigger/smaller than that _____." Students complete the sentences while making a gesture, e.g., "This ship is bigger than this yacht." (Makes a 'big' gesture, then a 'small' gesture). "The ruler is like a small canal." (Draws a line with their finger).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Help Me Understand."</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair has a simple "homework instruction" (e.g., "Describe a vessel crossing the Canal") and a related picture. Student A reads the instruction and pretends to not understand a word (e.g., 'vessel'). Student B must assist by pointing to the picture and explaining: "This is a vessel! It's like a big boat. It travels through the Canal." Student B can also mimic steering a boat to help. They swap roles.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting the Panama Canal"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative mini-presentation with peer assistance for the "Virtual Canal Tour" project. Students work in small groups (3-4). Each group has a poster or a set of pictures prepared for their "Virtual Canal Tour." One student acts as the primary presenter, giving a short "tour" (e.g., pointing to the Canal on a map, describing a vessel, mentioning the locks). Another student in the group acts as a "mediator" or "helper" who assists by:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pointing to visuals when the presenter mentions something (e.g., "This is the Canal. It connects two oceans." Helper points).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarifying or rephrasing a simple concept if a peer from the audience looks confused (e.g., "He means the Atlantic and Pacific are very big water areas.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Adding a comparison with gestures (e.g., "This ship is bigger than that boat," with accompanying gestures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Mimicking actions to help explain (e.g., pretending to steer a vessel).</li>
                </ul>
                The primary presenter uses present simple, present continuous, and comparatives. For example: "This is a vessel. It travels through the locks. Look! Two vessels are crossing now. This black vessel is bigger than that white ship." The helper ensures audience understanding through mediation strategies.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes groups during their mini-presentations, specifically noting the "mediator" student's ability to assist peers and facilitate communication. Students will complete a short peer assessment of their group members' mediation skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (canal, vessel, ocean, locks, connect, big, small, travel) is intelligible.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of explanations:</b> Explanations are simple and easy to understand for peers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pointing to relevant visuals:</b> Assists classmates by accurately pointing to pictures/map.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comparing with gestures:</b> Uses gestures to help compare objects (e.g., 'like a big water road').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Facilitating communication:</b> Mimics actions (e.g., steering a vessel) to aid understanding.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I help my friends understand the Panama Canal today? Yes / No"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy/difficult when explaining to my friends?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new things did I learn about helping my classmates understand?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I use gestures and pointing more to explain things?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Today, students effectively practiced mediation skills by assisting peers during the Virtual Canal Tour presentations. The use of visuals and gestures was very helpful. Some students still need more practice with clear, simple explanations and using comparatives. Adjustments for next session: Provide more structured sentence starters for comparisons and explicit practice in rephrasing for clarity. Emphasize the /k/ and /b/ sounds again during feedback. Continue to build confidence for the final project presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the specific theme. The next lesson will transition to a new theme, but the mediation skills practiced will be transferable to understanding and explaining new concepts and project assignments.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>